course: dictionary
lesson: 3521
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it show you which one is meant. The word favour is a good example. «Could you do me a favour?» means a kind thing you do to help someone.
explain: «Some rules are unfair, for example a rule that favours one group.» Here favours means treats one group better than others. The word «unfair» before it is the clue, because a favour in this sense is not fair to everyone.
explain: Now look at «All in favour» in a class vote. It means everyone who agrees, so favour means supporting an idea. To choose the right meaning, ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then swap it in and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A favour is a kind thing you do to help someone, as in «Could you do me a favour?»
fact: «In favour of» means supporting or agreeing with something.
fact: To favour someone is to treat them better than others, which is often unfair.
fact: A party favour is a small gift for guests.
fact: In American English, favour is spelled favor.
exercise: Kofi asks, «Could you do me a favour?» What is he asking for? | A ride on a bike | A party gift to take home | A test on his spelling | *A kind thing to help him
exercise: «Some rules are unfair, for example a rule that favours one group.» What does favours mean here? | Asks a group for help | *Treats one group better than others | Gives a small gift to each group | Does a kind thing for a group
exercise: Which phrase means supporting or agreeing with something? | Do someone a favour | Party favour | *In favour of | Return the favour
test: «Friends sometimes pay for each other; you can pay back with a favour, a drawing or being a good mate.» In this sentence, what is a favour? | A type of drawing | *A kind deed you do for a friend | A rule that is unfair | A gift for a party guest
test: In a class vote, «all in favour» means raising your hand. What does in favour mean? | Standing at the back of the room | *Supporting or agreeing with the idea | Losing the vote | Feeling sleepy about the idea
exam: Which sentence uses favour to mean treat someone better than others? | «Could you do me a favour?» | «All in favour, raise your hands.» | «Take a party favour home.» | *«The teacher should not favour one pupil.»
